---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Reading from '../../../layouts/Reading.astro';

// The two photographs that already exist in the repo and belong here. Both
// were shot for the about page; a page about cooking that shows the cooking
// is worth more than a page about cooking that shows nothing.
import sundayLunch from '../../../assets/portrait/kevin-holler-2.jpg';
import theGarden from '../../../assets/portrait/landscaping.jpg';

// The side column is 24rem (384px) at full page width.
const sideWidths = [384, 768];
const sideSizes = '(max-width: 960px) 100vw, 384px';
---
<Reading
  title="Food & Wine — Kevin Holler"
  description="Cooking, sourdough, growing, and winemaking — the part of Kevin Holler's week that has nothing to do with a screen."
  here="food"
  layout="spread"
>
  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h1>Food &amp; wine</h1>
      <p class="stamp">Cooking, sourdough, growing, and a first go at wine.</p>

      <p class="lede">
        The kitchen experiments have escalated to the point where I keep a
        tasting journal. That is either a hobby or a diagnosis, and I have
        stopped trying to work out which.
      </p>

      <p>
        All of this is the same instinct that makes the rest of the site what
        it is: take something you do not understand, do it badly, write down
        what happened, and do it again. It is just that the feedback loop is
        measured in hours or seasons rather than in deploys, and you can feed
        the result to people you like.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="spread-side">
      <figure class="figure">
        <Image
          src={sundayLunch}
          alt="Cooking for friends at home."
          widths={sideWidths}
          sizes={sideSizes}
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
        <figcaption>Sunday lunch · home</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h2>Sourdough</h2>
      <p>
        A starter, a scale, and several years of arguing with the same loaf.
        Bread is the most honest thing I make: the variables are few enough to
        hold in your head and unforgiving enough that you cannot bluff a bad
        one. Hydration, temperature, time, and the fact that the kitchen in
        February is a different kitchen than the one in August.
      </p>
      <p>
        TODO — your actual formula, the starter's age and name if it has one,
        and the one thing you would tell someone on their first loaf. That
        last one is the paragraph people will screenshot.
      </p>

      <h2>Cooking</h2>
      <p>
        Mostly for other people. There is a rotating dinner club, a Sunday
        lunch habit, and a tasting journal that started as a way to stop
        making the same mistake twice and turned into a record of what a year
        actually tasted like.
      </p>
      <p>
        TODO — the handful of dishes you would cook for anyone, and where the
        German-Irish household lands on a menu. Specifics beat adjectives
        here: one dish described properly is worth a paragraph of
        "seasonal".
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="spread-side"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h2>Growing</h2>
      <p>
        What the garden produces mostly decides what gets cooked, which is a
        better constraint than any recipe book. This year's project is a small
        orchard — which means the interesting part of it is four years away,
        and that is fine.
      </p>
      <p>
        The beds and the built work are on
        <a href="/new/gardening/">gardening &amp; landscaping</a>. This page is
        only what comes out of them.
      </p>
      <p>TODO — what is actually in the ground, and what has reliably failed.</p>

      <h2>Wine</h2>
      <p>
        The newest of the four and the one I am worst at. Fermentation is the
        same problem as sourdough with a much longer feedback loop and no
        chance to try again until next autumn, which makes the notes matter
        more than the technique.
      </p>
      <p>
        TODO — what you have pressed, how much of it, what went wrong, and
        whether any of it is drinkable. A first vintage described honestly is
        a far better page than a second vintage described well.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="spread-side">
      <figure class="figure">
        <Image
          src={theGarden}
          alt="The garden at home, with a playhouse and a bench."
          widths={sideWidths}
          sizes={sideSizes}
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Where most of it starts · Würzburg</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</Reading>
